Log — Day 3

1. Actual duration: 1h30 — malloc + struct + reflection on double free.

2. New tools/commands: malloc, free, struct, scanf("%zu", ...) to read size_t, NULL as a sentinel pointer value.

3. Bug + root cause: free_signal trying to free() signal.data[i] (a double, not a pointer) instead of free()-ing the entire data pointer — confusion between the old pattern (array of pointers, one malloc per element) and the current pattern (a single malloc for the whole array); invalid syntax output.*data (C++ pointer-to-member operator, doesn't exist in C) — confusion between C and C++ learned 10 years ago; create_signal function missing a return on the original success path (from commenting out the error returns without knowing what to put there instead).

4. Hypothesis tested and corrected: I thought break would solve the problem of "exiting early" from the function on error — actually break only exits loops, not functions; the right tool was return, I just hadn't decided what to return. I still had to decide how to signal an error in a function that returns a struct by value (no obvious NULL like with pointers) — solved with the "sentinel struct" strategy (data = NULL, length = 0).

5. Confidence at checkpoints: low on error-handling design (had no idea how to solve it on my own, needed the 3 options presented to pick one) — but high on the implementation after choosing the strategy, including finding the remaining bug of length not being zeroed on the malloc failure branch on my own.

6. Open questions: none pending.

7. Connection to the project: struct Signal { double *data; size_t length; } is the direct embryo of a variable-size signal abstraction — it solves the problem that static arrays (Day 1/2) didn't: a real OFDM signal has a size that depends on configuration (FFT size, subcarriers), not fixed at compile time.

8. Source of help: old C++ documentation (which confused the syntax, causing the .*data error); Copilot used minimally; most of the error-handling design came from conceptual discussion before coding, not external search.
